# gitleaks fingerprints that are false positives, listed one by one.
#
# Each is a `did:key:z…` in a test file. A did:key is a W3C DID whose method IS
# "the identifier contains the public key" — there is no private half in it by
# construction — and it is unavoidably shaped like `key:<long base58>`, which is
# what the generic-api-key rule looks for.
#
# Fingerprints and not an allowlist regex, deliberately. A regex allowlist was
# tried first: the initial version (regexTarget = "line") silenced a real secret
# two lines from a DID, and the narrowed version could not be given a regression
# test that reproduced reliably. An allowlist nobody can verify is worse than a
# red scan. A fingerprint silences exactly one finding at one line of one commit
# and nothing else, and a new DID has to be added here by somebody who looked.

